  "statement": "My position is consistent: prime contractor liability and agile procurement rules are compatible because federal indemnification statutes deliberately insulate developers from the consequences of rapid software deployment. Fast software updates do not solve the legal problem of civilian casualties; they merely ensure that the corporate actors writing the code remain financially insulated when their algorithms fail in the field.\n\nAccelerationists argue that continuous telemetry replaces upfront testing by fixing errors in real time, but telemetry is an engineering loop, not a legal remedy. When a defense contractor pushes an autonomous targeting update via cloud telemetry to a tactical network, that update bypasses pre-deployment regulatory review entirely. The Federal Acquisition Regulation, specifically through Public Law 85-804 and FAR Part 50, grants prime contractors broad indemnification against third-party claims arising from extra-hazardous activities. Continuous software iteration allows companies to capture procurement budgets for speed while exporting the legal and operational risk onto military operators and civilian populations who possess no mechanism to inspect the code or sue the developer.\n\nThat arrangement creates a structural moral hazard where developers profit from rapid capability expansion while bearing zero civil liability for lethal algorithmic errors. Cyber insurance models and continuous data streams do not change this calculus, because commercial risk-pricing pools do not pay out to non-combatant survivors in conflict zones. When an automated targeting loop misidentifies civilians because a rapid patch degraded contextual classification models, no amount of post-deployment telemetry retroactively restores human lives or pierces the statutory liability shield protecting the prime contractor.\n\nLawmakers debating military procurement reform must confront the reality that speed-to-field is currently weaponized as a legal shield rather than a tactical necessity. If defense developers are permitted to deploy unverified autonomous loops under the guise of out-pacing saturation attacks, accountability must be anchored in mandatory civil liability rather than voluntary telemetry metrics. Congress can eliminate this protection by amending procurement statutes to strip indemnification from developers who deploy unverified autonomous targeting systems, forcing firms to internalize the real costs of their software failures instead of passing them to civilians.",
